<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">Autumn 218 BCE · Northern Italy</p><h1 class="sp-title">Hannibal to His Army before the Ticinus</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Having crossed the Alps with a weakened army in 218 BCE, Hannibal faced his first battle against Rome on the banks of the Ticinus in northern Italy. Livy describes him rousing his men by telling them they were trapped between the mountains and the enemy, with nothing behind them to fall back on.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Here, soldiers, you must conquer or die.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Livy, History of Rome, XXI.43–44, rendering the Latin 'hic vincendum aut moriendum'.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/From_the_Founding_of_the_City/Book_21#43" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource, tr. Canon Roberts</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Hannibal's cavalry won at the Ticinus, and within two years he had destroyed Roman armies at the Trebia, Lake Trasimene and Cannae. Yet he never took Rome, and the war ended with his defeat at Zama in 202 BCE.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trebia">Battle of the Trebia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-trasimene">Battle of Lake Trasimene</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a></div></div></section>
